<header class="page-header">
  <p class="page-header__eyebrow">Topic</p>
  <h1 class="page-header__title">Into the forest for mushrooms</h1>
  <p class="page-header__lead">
    A sane entry point into foraging: learn the season first,
    then the species, then — deliberately — the dangerous
    lookalikes. This route is built around the idea that a
    mushroom is identified by a full set of traits, never by
    one photo.
  </p>
</header>

<section>
  <h2>First — the time of year</h2>
  <p>
    The <a href="{{ '/en/sezon/' | url }}">seasonal calendar</a>
    helps you pick which species to study right now. The more
    detailed <a href="{{ '/en/stati/sezonnyj-kalendar-gribnika/' | url }}">month-by-month guide</a>
    is written for the temperate belt — its geographic frame
    matters.
  </p>

  <h2>Then — the species description</h2>
  <p>
    The <a href="{{ '/en/griby/' | url }}">mushroom catalog</a>
    holds cards with descriptions, photos, habitat notes and
    source links. Compare the full set of traits: cap, gills or
    pores, stem, ring, volva, flesh colour changes, smell, spore
    print — plus where and when it grows. A photograph, or an
    app identification from a single shot, does not replace a
    reliable species determination.
  </p>

  <h2>Separately — the dangerous lookalikes</h2>
  <p>
    Study lookalike pairs together, not only the mushroom you
    hope to find. A good first route is to open the cards of the
    <a href="{{ '/en/griby/borovik/' | url }}">porcini</a>,
    the <a href="{{ '/en/griby/zhelchnyj-grib/' | url }}">bitter bolete</a>
    and the <a href="{{ '/en/griby/satanskij-grib/' | url }}">devil's bolete</a>,
    then follow the source links inside each card.
  </p>
  <p class="topic-note">
    If a specimen raises any doubt, do not eat it. The site's
    materials help you learn — they cannot confirm the safety of
    a specific mushroom you have picked.
  </p>
</section>

